Custom Shell System is a wardrobe for Mortal Shell II. Wear a different look without touching your shell's abilities: outfits people have made for CSS, the game's own shells, or any enemy and NPC built on the player's rig. Change colours, hide parts, tune the physics, save profiles, and it all lives in the game's Inventory menu, drawn with the game's own widgets.

[b]CSS is the runtime. Install UE4SS and the outfit packages you want separately.[/b] The outfits in the screenshots are not in this download.

[size=4][b]What it does[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Shell tab.[/b] Your installed outfits, favorites on top, with thumbnails. Wear a variant, or wear an official shell's look, or one of 69 enemies, people and Harbinger forms with Use NPC / Enemy.[/*]
[*][b]Customize.[/b] Whatever the outfit exposes: templates and palettes, colour swatches or exact RGB, part toggles, texture choices, body shape sliders, glow and opacity, and motion presets for chest, glutes, thighs, belly and hair. Ground height for outfits whose feet sink or float.[/*]
[*][b]Locomotion.[/b] Idle, walk, jog, sprint and beacon animations per outfit, where the outfit ships them, plus CSS's feminine walk.[/*]
[*][b]Misc.[/b] Hide seals, sidearms, stowed weapons and accessories, always or only while you use them, and each item your current shell carries (Gragu's heart, Eredrim's bell, Genessa's burner) on its own rule.[/*]
[*][b]Profile.[/b] Save the whole character (outfit, colours, shapes, physics, animations, Misc rules) under a name and load it back later.[/*]
[*][b]Harbinger.[/b] Sever mid-fight and your look comes with you.[/*]
[/list]

CSS keeps its own state next to the mod, separate from the game save. You don't need every shell, weapon or seal unlocked.

[size=4][b]Install[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Close the game.[/*]
[*]Install [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/mortalshell2/mods/45?tab=files]UE4SS for MS2 by Framecore[/url]. CSS uses UE4SS v3.0.1-1028-gd7e7826d, included in the verified main-file bundle version 1.1.[/*]
[*]Extract the CustomShellSystem folder from the CSS ZIP into MortalShell2/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/.[/*]
[*]Put your CSS outfit packages in MortalShell2/Content/Paks/~mods/, following each outfit's own instructions.[/*]
[*]Enter the game world, open [b]Inventory[/b] (default [b]I[/b]) and pick the [b]CSS[/b] tab. Choose an outfit, Wear.[/*]
[/list]

Check that the loader is at [b]ue4ss/Mods/CustomShellSystem/dlls/main.dll[/b] with no extra nested folder. Keep each outfit's .pak, .utoc and .ucas together with their _P suffix. Restart the game after adding or replacing outfit packages.

[size=4][b]Controls[/b][/size]

The page shows the prompts for whatever you're holding, keyboard or pad, and every prompt you see can be clicked.

[b]Mouse and keyboard[/b]
[list]
[*]I opens the Inventory. Q / E move between the game's tabs, Z / X between CSS's own (Shell, Customize, Locomotion, Misc, Profile).[/*]
[*]W / S move in the list, A / D change the thing on the right (variant, colour, slider, preset).[/*]
[*]Space is the main action (Wear, Reset part, Load profile). F is the second one (Search, next channel, Exact colour). C is the third (favorite, Reset all, delete).[/*]
[*]Click any row, tab or prompt. Drag a slider's bar. Scroll the lists and the details window.[/*]
[*]Over the character: right-drag rotates, wheel zooms, left-drag moves the framing. Home resets the view, I toggles the lighting controls. Esc closes.[/*]
[/list]

[b]Controller[/b]
[list]
[*]LB / RB for the game's tabs, LT / RT for CSS's tabs.[/*]
[*]D-pad up / down for the list, left / right for the value on the right.[/*]
[*]A wears or confirms, X is the second action, Y the third. B closes.[/*]
[*]Right stick rotates and zooms, left stick moves the framing, right-stick click resets the view, Y (on the character) toggles lighting.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Updating and compatibility[/b][/size]

Close the game and replace the CSS runtime files. [b]Keep CustomShellSystem/state/[/b]: favorites, profiles, colours and Misc rules live there. The ZIP has no personal state, one loader and one core DLL.

Install outfits marked CSS-compatible. A plain mesh replacer doesn't show up in the wardrobe by itself, and a replacer that overwrites the game's base meshes or materials can affect CSS outfits that use those assets. Every CSS outfit package published so far still works with beta.5.

If something breaks, send me your CSS and UE4SS versions, the outfit, your gameplay shell, the other mods you run and how to reproduce it. A screenshot or clip helps a lot. To remove CSS, close the game and delete CustomShellSystem from ue4ss/Mods/. Outfit packages are separate and come out of ~mods on their own.

[size=4][b]For modders[/b][/size]

CSS is a framework: the parts, colours, shapes, presets and animations you see in Customize all come from the outfit's manifest, and there's a written convention for what an outfit declares (roles like garment, metal, gem, skin, hair; which keep their hue; which group they tint with). Packages from before the convention keep working, CSS infers it from the control ids.

[url=https://github.com/ngodn/CustomShellSystem/blob/main/docs/modding/README.md]Modding guide[/url] | [url=https://github.com/ngodn/CustomShellSystem/blob/main/docs/control-convention.md]Control convention[/url] | [url=https://github.com/ngodn/CustomShellSystem/blob/main/docs/css-packages.md]Package format[/url]

The [url=https://github.com/ngodn/CustomShellSystem]source[/url] and the [url=https://github.com/ngodn/CustomShellSystem/blob/main/tools/css_convert.py]Python converter[/url] are on GitHub. The converter takes cooked Mortal Shell II .pak/.utoc/.ucas files and builds a CSS package with metadata, a thumbnail and optional colour recipes:

[code]python3 tools/css_convert.py "/path/to/original-mod" --name "My Outfit" --author "YourName" --id yourname.myoutfit --thumbnail "/path/to/thumbnail.png" --game "/path/to/MortalShell2" --retoc "/path/to/retoc" --repak "/path/to/repak" --output "./dist"[/code]

Square PNG for the thumbnail, 512 x 512 works. Keep the same package id when you update. Some mods need work before they behave: material-slot mappings, missing dependencies, skeleton compatibility, colour masks. The converter won't invent colour regions or fit a mesh from another game onto Mortal Shell II's rig; that's the [url=https://github.com/ngodn/CustomShellSystem/blob/main/docs/porting-variants.md]variant workflow[/url]. Test in game before you share, and keep the original authors credited.

The NPC roster is a JSON file in the mod folder (catalog/npc-appearances.css.json) if you want to add or remove characters. Only meshes on the player's skeleton work; CSS checks on every wear.

